<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ernest I, Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1497">1497</a>–<a href="/years/1546">1546</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Ernest the Confessor · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-welf">House of Welf</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ernest I, called the Confessor, was Duke of Brunswick-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneburg">Lüneburg</a>, ruling the Principality of Lüneburg from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celle">Celle</a> from 1520. A former student at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wittenberg">Wittenberg</a>, he introduced the Lutheran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> from 1527, signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg-confession">Augsburg Confession</a> in 1530 and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schmalkaldic-league">Schmalkaldic League</a>. He also restored the finances of his heavily indebted principality.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ernest-i-duke-of-brunswick-luneburg.jpg" alt="Ernest I, Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg" width="320" height="473" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Workshop of Lucas Cranach the Elder · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lucas_Cranach_d.%C3%84._(Werkst.)_-_Ernst_der_Bekenner,_Herzog_von_Braunschweig-L%C3%BCneburg-Celle.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ernest was born at Uelzen on 27 June 1497 into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-welf">House of Guelph</a>, the son of Henry I, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneburg">Lüneburg</a>, and Margarete of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, a sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-iii-elector-of-saxony">Frederick the Wise</a>, Elector of Saxony. In 1512 he was sent to his uncle's court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wittenberg">Wittenberg</a>, where Georg Spalatin taught him at the university, and he was still there when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> began. In 1520 political friction with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> led his father to abdicate and leave for the French court, and Ernest and his elder brother Otto ruled jointly until Otto retired in 1527.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Ernest at first moved slowly. The Peasants' War of 1525 gave him and his brother occasion to make the monasteries declare their property and admit Protestant preachers, and after a Catholic attempt to restore his father failed in 1527 his course became firmer. In July 1527 a book of discipline drawn up by the preachers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celle">Celle</a> was adopted, and a diet that August ordered God's pure word to be preached everywhere. By 1530 Lutheran preachers served most parishes and monasteries, not always without compulsion. Ernest brought Urbanus Rhegius back from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> reached the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneburg">Lüneburg</a>; St. Michael's, the richest monastery, accepted it in 1532.</p><p>After 1530 Ernest was the most influential prince of North Germany. He sent Rhegius to restore order in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>, strengthened the Protestant party in the cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a>, though without success in Münster, and drew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottingen">Göttingen</a> and other cities into the Schmalkald League, often mediating between the cautious Elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> and the headstrong Philip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He married Sophia, daughter of Henry V, Duke of Mecklenburg, at Schwerin on 2 June 1528; their sons included Francis Otto, Henry and William, later dukes. At his death his four sons were still minors, but the Lutheran church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneburg">Lüneburg</a> was by then firm enough to survive the regency and the Schmalkald War. He sometimes used harsh measures and sought to raise his own position, yet he is remembered by his motto, consumed in service of others, and as Ernest the Confessor.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ernest I of Brunswick” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Duke <b>Ernest the Confessor</b> introduced the Reformation from 1527.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-luneburg">Principality of Lüneburg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Uelzen, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celle">Celle</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.97,10.57&amp;year=1528" aria-label="Uelzen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4786 339 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ernest I, Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5236 609h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5236 609h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.97,10.57&amp;year=1528">What was here in 1528? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
